WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:04.770
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:09.710
Ever thought about the fact that we have a
stupendously large amount of unlabeled videos

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:10.750
on the internet?

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:16.650
And, of course, with the ascendancy of machine
learning algorithms that can learn by themselves,

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:21.490
it would be a huge missed opportunity to not
make any use of all this free data.

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:26.809
This is a crazy piece of work where the idea
is to unleash a neural network on a large

00:00:26.809 --> 00:00:32.570
number of publicly uploaded videos on the
internet, and see how well it does if we ask

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:35.250
it to generate new videos from scratch.

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:40.670
Here, unlabeled means there is no information
as to what we see in these videos, they are

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:42.870
just provided as-is.

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:47.530
Machine learning methods that work on this
kind of data, we like to call unsupervised

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:48.960
learning techniques.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:52.910
This work is based on a generative adversarial
network.

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:55.160
Wait, what does this mean exactly?

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:59.690
This means that we have two neural networks
that race each other, where one tries to generate

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:05.170
more and more real-looking animations, and
passes it over to the other that learns to

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:07.890
tell real footage from fake ones.

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:12.940
The first we call the generator network, and
the second is the discriminator network.

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:18.100
They try to outperform each other, and this
rivalry goes on for quite a while and improves

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:24.640
the quality of output for both neural networks,
hence the name, generative adversarial networks.

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:29.950
At first, we have covered this concept that
was used to generate images from written text

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:30.950
descriptions.

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:35.960
The shortcoming of this approach was the slow
training time that led to extremely tiny,

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:38.119
low resolution output images.

00:01:38.119 --> 00:01:43.490
This was remedied by a followup work which
proposed a two-stage version of this architecture.

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:48.050
We have covered this in an earlier Two Minute
Papers episode, as always, the link is available

00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:49.580
in the video description.

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:54.220
It would not be an understatement to say that
I nearly fell off the chair when seeing these

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:55.680
incredible results.

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:57.810
So, where do we go from here?

00:01:57.810 --> 00:01:59.760
What shall be the next step?

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:01.830
Well, of course, video!

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:06.210
However, the implementation of such a technique
is far from trivial.

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:10.840
In this piece of work, the generator network
learns not on the original representation

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:16.829
of the videos, but on the foreground and background
video streams separately, and it also has

00:02:16.829 --> 00:02:21.239
to learn what combination of these yields
realistic footage.

00:02:21.239 --> 00:02:25.969
This two-stream architecture is particularly
useful in modeling real-world videos where

00:02:25.969 --> 00:02:31.209
the background is mostly stationary and there
is an animated movement in the foreground.

00:02:31.209 --> 00:02:36.609
A train passing the station or people playing
golf on the field are excellent examples of

00:02:36.609 --> 00:02:38.299
this kind of separation.

00:02:38.299 --> 00:02:43.379
We definitely need a high quality discriminator
network as well, as in the final synthesized

00:02:43.379 --> 00:02:48.730
footage, not only the foreground and background
must go well together, but the synthesized

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:52.590
animations also have to be believable for
human beings.

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:57.319
This human being is, in our case, is represented
by the discriminator network.

00:02:57.319 --> 00:03:01.849
Needless to say, this problem is extremely
difficult and the quality of the discriminator

00:03:01.849 --> 00:03:04.709
network makes or breaks this magic trick.

00:03:04.709 --> 00:03:09.150
And of course, the all important question
immediately arises: if there are multiple

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:14.939
algorithms performing this action, how do
we decide which one is the best?

00:03:14.939 --> 00:03:19.719
Generally, we get a few people, and show them
a piece of synthesized footage with this algorithm

00:03:19.719 --> 00:03:24.409
and previous works, and have them decide which
they deem more realistic.

00:03:24.409 --> 00:03:29.370
This is still the first step - I expect these
techniques to improve so rapidly that we'll

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:33.129
soon find ourselves testing against real-world
footage.

00:03:33.129 --> 00:03:37.709
And who knows, sometimes perhaps failing to
recognize which is which.

00:03:37.709 --> 00:03:42.029
The results in the paper show that this new
technique beats the previous techniques by

00:03:42.029 --> 00:03:46.670
a significant margin, and that users have
a strong preference towards the two-stream

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:47.670
architecture.

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:51.700
The previous technique they compare against
is an autoencoder, which we have discussed

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:56.900
in a previous Two Minute Papers episode, check
it out, it is available in the video description!

00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:01.659
The disadvantages of this approach are quite
easy to identify this time around: we have

00:04:01.659 --> 00:04:08.629
a very limited resolution for these output
video streams, that is, 64x64 pixels for 32

00:04:08.629 --> 00:04:14.279
frames, which, even at modest framerate, is
just slightly over one second of footage.

00:04:14.279 --> 00:04:19.200
The synthesized results vary greatly in quality,
but it is remarkable to see that the machine

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:24.840
can have a rough understanding of the concept
of a large variety of movement and animation

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:25.840
types.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:30.290
It is really incredible to see that the neural
network learns about the representations of

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:36.040
these objects and how they move, even when
it wasn't explicitly instructed to do so.

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:39.260
We can also visualize what the neural network
has learned.

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:45.040
This is done by finding different image inputs
that make a particular neuron extremely excited.

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:50.590
Here, we see a collection of inputs including
these activations for images of people and

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:51.590
trains.

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:55.750
The authors' website is definitely worthy
of checking out as some of the submenus are

00:04:55.750 --> 00:04:57.570
quite ample in results.

00:04:57.570 --> 00:05:03.900
Some amazing, some, well, a bit horrifying,
but what is sure is that all of them are quite

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:04.900
interesting.

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:09.750
And before we go, a huge shoutout to László
Csöndes, who helped us quite a bit in sorting

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:12.400
out a number of technical issues with the
series.

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:16.320
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

